WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.349
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.649 --> 00:00:10.279
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.579 --> 00:00:17.922
Unit ten: Case Study Reports. Today you will add background compactly with appositives and reduced relative clauses.

4
00:00:18.222 --> 00:00:20.828
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:25.023
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.883 --> 00:00:27.082
Case background.

7
00:00:28.083 --> 00:00:32.126
Case background means the setting and history that explain why a case matters.

8
00:00:32.717 --> 00:00:35.691
The case background describes the firm's early years.

9
00:00:36.283 --> 00:00:39.005
Your turn. Say: Case background.

10
00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:43.170
Case background.

11
00:00:43.917 --> 00:00:45.278
Data collection.

12
00:00:46.283 --> 00:00:51.000
Data collection means the gathering of information through interviews, surveys or records.

13
00:00:51.583 --> 00:00:53.792
Data collection lasted six months.

14
00:00:54.383 --> 00:00:57.245
Your turn. Say: Data collection.

15
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.519
Data collection.

16
00:01:02.283 --> 00:01:03.760
Analytical framework.

17
00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:09.153
Analytical framework means a set of ideas used to organise and interpret the evidence.

18
00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.609
The analytical framework came from economics.

19
00:01:13.217 --> 00:01:16.426
Your turn. Say: Analytical framework.

20
00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:20.721
Analytical framework.

21
00:01:21.483 --> 00:01:22.775
Findings summary.

22
00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:27.014
Findings summary means a short statement of the main results.

23
00:01:27.617 --> 00:01:30.023
The findings summary fits on one page.

24
00:01:30.617 --> 00:01:33.768
Your turn. Say: Findings summary.

25
00:01:36.633 --> 00:01:37.925
Findings summary.

26
00:01:38.683 --> 00:01:39.998
Recommendation.

27
00:01:40.983 --> 00:01:44.794
Recommendation means a suggestion about what should be done next.

28
00:01:45.383 --> 00:01:48.021
The final recommendation was to train staff.

29
00:01:48.617 --> 00:01:51.385
Your turn. Say: Recommendation.

30
00:01:54.233 --> 00:01:55.618
Recommendation.

31
00:01:56.383 --> 00:01:58.209
Real-world application.

32
00:01:59.217 --> 00:02:03.329
Real-world application means the use of an idea in actual practice.

33
00:02:03.917 --> 00:02:06.694
A real-world application makes the case useful.

34
00:02:07.283 --> 00:02:10.505
Your turn. Say: Real-world application.

35
00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:15.598
Real-world application.

36
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.117
Stakeholder analysis.

37
00:02:19.117 --> 00:02:24.204
Stakeholder analysis means the study of everyone affected by or involved in a decision.

38
00:02:24.817 --> 00:02:28.755
Stakeholder analysis listed workers, owners and customers.

39
00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:32.827
Your turn. Say: Stakeholder analysis.

40
00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:37.376
Stakeholder analysis.

41
00:02:38.117 --> 00:02:39.559
Limitations section.

42
00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:45.069
Limitations section means the part of a report that admits what the study could not do.

43
00:02:45.683 --> 00:02:48.577
The limitations section noted the small sample.

44
00:02:49.183 --> 00:02:52.567
Your turn. Say: Limitations section.

45
00:02:55.433 --> 00:02:57.061
Limitations section.

46
00:02:57.867 --> 00:03:01.448
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:02.283 --> 00:03:12.192
Visal, a report says 'The company, a family firm founded in 1990, faced rising costs.' What is 'a family firm founded in 1990'?

48
00:03:13.283 --> 00:03:23.984
An appositive. A noun phrase placed beside a noun to identify it. Commas mark the extra information, and it saves a whole sentence of background.

49
00:03:25.083 --> 00:03:29.068
What about 'The data collected in 2023 show a decline'?

50
00:03:30.183 --> 00:03:43.717
That is a reduced relative clause. 'Which were collected' becomes 'collected'. Use a past participle for passive meaning and an -ing form for active meaning: workers earning low wages.

51
00:03:44.817 --> 00:03:45.818
Any warning?

52
00:03:46.917 --> 00:03:59.356
Appositives need commas only when they add extra detail. Reduced clauses must still be clear: the data collected show a decline, with plural verb agreement for data.

53
00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:01.884
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:06.886
Grammar focus: Appositives and reduced relative clauses.

55
00:04:07.186 --> 00:04:08.338
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:14.393
The company, a family firm founded in 1990, faced rising costs.

57
00:04:15.283 --> 00:04:19.106
The data collected in 2023 show a clear decline.

58
00:04:20.017 --> 00:04:23.224
The factory employing three hundred workers closed last year.

59
00:04:24.167 --> 00:04:34.435
An appositive identifies a noun with extra detail between commas. A reduced relative clause drops who / which + be: collected (passive) or employing (active).

60
00:04:35.183 --> 00:04:40.599
Be careful! Don't say: The company, founded 1990, grew fast.

61
00:04:42.533 --> 00:04:47.342
Say: The company, founded in 1990, grew fast.

62
00:04:47.642 --> 00:04:49.131
Use in before the year.

63
00:04:49.933 --> 00:04:52.644
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:53.433 --> 00:04:54.609
Setting the Scene.

65
00:04:54.909 --> 00:05:00.600
A case study report begins with the case background, a short account of why the situation matters.

66
00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.328
The data collected through interviews and records were organised using an analytical framework.

67
00:05:07.333 --> 00:05:13.466
The stakeholder analysis, a list of everyone affected by the decision, helped the team judge the risks.

68
00:05:14.467 --> 00:05:20.205
A findings summary leading to a clear recommendation gives the report a real-world application.

69
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:26.485
The limitations section, written honestly, tells readers how far the results can be trusted.

70
00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.277
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:32.117 --> 00:05:34.894
The report blank last year was very clear.

72
00:05:35.194 --> 00:05:39.115
A, published. B, publish. C, was publishing it.

73
00:05:42.667 --> 00:05:44.956
The answer is A: published.

74
00:05:46.117 --> 00:05:50.148
The part of a report that admits what a study could not do is the blank section.

75
00:05:50.448 --> 00:05:54.589
A, limitations. B, findings. C, stakeholder.

76
00:05:58.133 --> 00:06:00.353
The answer is A: limitations.

77
00:06:01.517 --> 00:06:04.248
Which sentence uses an appositive correctly?

78
00:06:04.548 --> 00:06:13.019
A, Mr Sok, a local teacher, led the study.. B, Mr Sok a local teacher led, the study.. C, Mr Sok, a local teacher led, the study..

79
00:06:16.567 --> 00:06:20.702
The answer is A: Mr Sok, a local teacher, led the study..

80
00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:24.790
A suggestion about what should be done next is a blank.

81
00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:28.999
A, limitation. B, recommendation. C, framework.

82
00:06:32.533 --> 00:06:34.707
The answer is B: recommendation.

83
00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:40.334
According to the reading, what gives a report a real-world application?

84
00:06:40.634 --> 00:06:46.157
A, a clear recommendation. B, a long title. C, a hidden appendix.

85
00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:52.303
The answer is A: a clear recommendation.

86
00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:56.652
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:57.517 --> 00:07:01.710
Why is it important for a case study report to include its own limitations?

88
00:07:10.517 --> 00:07:12.110
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:37.997
A case study report should include its limitations because no study is perfect. Readers need to know if the sample was small, if the data collection was short, or if the results may not apply elsewhere. Being honest about limitations builds trust and prevents readers from over-using the recommendation. It also shows other researchers where future work is needed.

90
00:07:38.297 --> 00:07:40.076
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:40.983 --> 00:07:46.190
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and appositives and reduced relative clauses.

92
00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:51.325
Today's value is analytical rigor. Test the numbers before you believe the story.

93
00:07:51.625 --> 00:07:54.173
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:54.473 --> 00:07:56.916
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

